    {
      "paper_id": "aos-2024-v52-i05-p2293",
      "local_id": "D2",
      "local_label": "Section 1 — Nonstationary causal representation (1.2)",
      "source_heading": "Section 1 — Nonstationary causal representation (1.2)",
      "source_kind": "definition",
      "statement_original": "To characterize the non-stationary process $(X_t)$, we view $X_t$ as outputs from a physical system with the following causal representation:\n\\[\nX_t=g_t(\\mathcal F_t),\\quad\\text{with }\\mathcal F_t=(\\ldots,\\varepsilon_{t-1},\\varepsilon_t),\n\\]\n(1.2)\nwhere $(\\varepsilon_i)_{i\\in\\mathbb Z}$ are i. i. d. inputs of this system and $g_t:\\mathbb R^\\infty\\to\\mathbb R$ are measurable functions.",
      "relation": "exact",
      "depends_on": [],
      "evidence": [
